UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 19, Lesson 2: Testing magnetic materials', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Magnetic materials are attracted to magnets (many iron/steel objects). Non-magnetic materials are not attracted even if they are metal.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same science idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA coin might be mixed alloys — how do you decide scientifically?\n\nFEEDBACK\nTest with a magnet and record the result rather than guessing from colour.\n\nWATCH FOR\nSorting by “looks shiny” is not a magnetic test.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nSort sixteen objects into magnetic/not using a magnet. Include three metals that are not magnetic if available. Write one surprise result.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Science and Technology","learning_intention":"Magnetic materials are attracted to magnets (many iron/steel objects). Non-magnetic materials are not attracted even if they are metal.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Apply and make","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe everyday objects for observation"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["ST2-SCI-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Sort sixteen objects into magnetic/not using a magnet. Include three metals that are not magnetic if available. Write one surprise result. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original demonstration: Sort: steel spoon (magnetic); plastic ruler (not); aluminium can (usually not); iron nail (magnetic).\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Sorting by “looks shiny” is not a magnetic test. Correction: Test with a magnet and record the result rather than guessing from colour.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Sorting by “looks shiny” is not a magnetic test. Keep sample answers out of the child role.","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Magnetic materials are attracted to magnets (many iron/steel objects). Non-magnetic materials are not attracted even if they are metal. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Magnetic materials are attracted to magnets (many iron/steel objects). Non-magnetic materials are not attracted even if they are metal.","Sorting by “looks shiny” is not a magnetic test.","Ignore the observation and invent a more exciting result.","Copy the model objects and call that independent work."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Understand and model\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, property or test step you used and say why it supports the claim. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Make the science idea visible: name the observation, property or test step you used and say why it supports the claim.","Writing only a final label without evidence does not show scientific thinking.","Copy the model objects and call that independent work.","Treat a missing measurement as if it were recorded."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Show the evidence\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.","Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Sorting by “looks shiny” is not a magnetic test.","Treat a missing measurement as if it were recorded.","Skip the fair-test idea because the first guess looked neat."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Test a misconception\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same science idea on a new object, material or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of observations. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use the same science idea on a new object, material or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of observations.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence.","Skip the fair-test idea because the first guess looked neat.","Ignore the observation and invent a more exciting result."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Transfer to a new investigation\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Test with a magnet and record the result rather than guessing from colour. Useful feedback uses the original observations and method.","options":["Test with a magnet and record the result rather than guessing from colour.","Sorting by “looks shiny” is not a magnetic test.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"A coin might be mixed alloys — how do you decide scientifically?\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label. Useful feedback uses the original observations and method.","options":["The most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","Writing only a final label without evidence does not show scientific thinking.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"List the key observations or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Test with a magnet and record the result rather than guessing from colour. Useful feedback uses the original observations and method.","options":["Test with a magnet and record the result rather than guessing from colour.","Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Sorting by “looks shiny” is not a magnetic test.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Describe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with e\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The new example should use the same science idea as the model, but contain different objects or data. Useful feedback uses the original observations and method.","options":["The new example should use the same science idea as the model, but contain different objects or data.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Investigate or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a s\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence, not repetition of the worked model, demonstrates the skill.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced relevant evidence with accurate reasoning and named observations.","question":"Investigate or describe a fresh example that uses the central skill. Show evidence and name the observations that matter.","sample_answer":"Original demonstration: Sort: steel spoon (magnetic); plastic ruler (not); aluminium can (usually not); iron nail (magnetic).","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A correction needs to change the claim or evidence, not just make the notes longer.","marking_guide":"Require an actual misconception plus corrected claim and an evidence-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely science misconception for this topic, correct it and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Common error: Sorting by “looks shiny” is not a magnetic test. Correction: Test with a magnet and record the result rather than guessing from colour.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-sci-y3-w19-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Science and Technology alignment using catalogue outcome codes; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise magnetic.","Read the mission before you start observing or testing.","Keep the property or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and 