.","explain":"Magnetic materials are attracted to magnets (many iron/steel objects). Non-magnetic materials are not attracted even if they are metal.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Sorting by “looks shiny” is not a magnetic test. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Magnetic materials test.","caption":"Shiny ≠ magnetic.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Test</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">use a magnet</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Sort</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">magnetic / not</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Sort: steel spoon (magnetic); plastic ruler (not); aluminium can (usually not); iron nail (magnetic). Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.\nJuno: Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Sort: steel spoon (magnetic); plastic ruler (not); aluminium can (usually not); iron nail (magnetic). Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, property or test step you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual observations and purpose of the task.","options":["Make the science idea visible: name the observation, property or test step you used and say why it supports the claim.","A claim unrelated to the observation or test.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSort: steel spoon (magnetic); plastic ruler (not); aluminium can (usually not); iron nail (magnetic). Look at each observation, variable or design choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, property or test step you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show scientific thinking. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Metal differences.","caption":"Record surprises.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Steel</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">usually yes</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Aluminium</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">usually no</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual observations and purpose of the task.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.","A claim unrelated to the observation or test.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSorting by “looks shiny” is not a magnetic test. Here is a more productive model: Sort: steel spoon (magnetic); plastic ruler (not); aluminium can (usually not); iron nail (magnetic).\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nTest with a magnet and record the result rather than guessing from colour.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Sorting by “looks shiny” is not a magnetic test. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same science idea on a new object, material or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of observations. Supported records follow the actual observations and purpose of the task.","options":["Use the same science idea on a new object, material or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of observations.","A claim unrelated to the observation or test.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nMagnetic materials are attracted to magnets (many iron/steel objects). Non-magnetic materials are not attracted even if they are metal. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInvestigate or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a